A significant vulnerability in machine learning models, known as "neural backdoors," allows malicious inputs to trigger specific undesirable behaviors when analyzed by compromised models. Existing approaches to creating neural backdoors typically involve modifying the model's weights, which can be time-consuming and resource-intensive. This work delves into whether introducing neural backdoors through adversarial weight modifications is feasible. By redefining the concept of adversarial weight modifications and developing a practical algorithm to implement it, the study sheds light on the challenges associated with this approach. Additionally, the research explores the potential of leveraging adversarial training to create neural backdoors and conducts a series of experiments to validate the effectiveness of the proposed method. This work is intended to inspire further investigation into the development of more efficient and stealthy neural backdoor attacks. The accompanying code can be accessed at https://github.com/ChenMingxiaoUT/Adversarial-Weight-Modification. \bigskip

